<Page title="Research · Declan Curran" description="Climate downscaling research and applied ML side projects by Declan Curran." image="/media/work/precipitation-var4-poster.jpg">
	<header class="page-head">
		<span class="kicker">research <span class="dot">·</span> questions before models</span>
		<h1>Research</h1>
		<p class="question">What would you do with a forecast for your own backyard?</p>
		<p class="lede">
			Mostly I turn data into decisions. I'm finishing a research Master's in Computer Science and
			tinkering with applied ML side projects.
		</p>
	</header>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-head">
			<span class="num">01</span>
			<h2>Climate downscaling with hierarchical denoising</h2>
		</div>
		<span class="kicker">research master's <span class="dot">·</span> computer science</span>
		<p>
			Global climate models are coarse, typically 25–100 km per grid cell. That's fine for global trends but
			useless if you need to know what's about to happen over your catchment, vineyard, or grid asset. My
			research uses a hierarchical denoising approach to take that coarse output and produce high-resolution
			predictions that match the underlying physics.
		</p>
		<figure>
			<video autoplay loop muted playsinline preload="metadata" poster="/media/work/precipitation-var4-poster.jpg">
				<source src="/media/work/precipitation-var4.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
			</video>
			<figcaption>
				Precipitation (var 4) over training: the model's prediction on the left and the residual against
				ground truth on the right. The prediction develops fine-scale structure as training goes on, while
				the residual stays small throughout.
			</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p class="tags mono">Downscaling · Diffusion & denoising · Climate models · Physical consistency</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-head">
			<span class="num">02</span>
			<h2>Fish-GPT</h2>
		</div>
		<span class="kicker">side project <span class="dot">·</span> vision + llm</span>
		<p>
			A toy that wires a vision model into a chat interface, so you can point a camera at a fish (or anything,
			really) and ask follow-up questions. I built it mainly to see what the failure modes of current
			vision-language models look like on natural, uncurated input.
		</p>
		<figure>
			<video controls preload="metadata" poster="/media/work/fish-gpt-poster.jpg" playsinline>
				<source src="/media/work/fish-gpt.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
			</video>
			<figcaption>Short demo: identification and Q&amp;A on a single fish image.</figcaption>
		</figure>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="section-head">
			<span class="num">03</span>
			<h2>Postcards from the field</h2>
		</div>
		<p>
			Sometimes the science leaks into the writing. <a href="/blog/voodoo-mould-and-models/">Voodoo, Mould, and
			Models</a> is a postcard from the American Geophysical Union conference in New Orleans.
		</p>
	</section>
</Page>

